{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And the life of Sarah was.</b> One needs to know why it says <b>וַיִּהְיוּ</b> and did not say according to the order stated for all the righteous: ״וַיְחִי אָדָם״ (Genesis 5:3), ״וַיְחִי שֵׁם״ (Genesis 11:11), or say ״אֵלֶּה שְׁנֵי״ according to the order stated for Abraham?" "<b>Indeed,</b> it may be explained along the lines of what they of blessed memory said (Tanhuma) that the cause of her death was when they informed her of the binding of Isaac and that he had nearly been slaughtered — her soul flew away. Accordingly, Sarah's death was on account of the report about Isaac. This is what he hinted by saying <b>וַיִּהְיוּ</b> — an expression of distress, alluding to what was told." "<b>Or</b> it may further intend, according to their statement of blessed memory (Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer 32) cited there, that she died by means of evil tidings. This would indicate that had the occurrence not happened she would still have had life — and this is his saying <b>וַיִּהְיוּ</b>, meaning a new, altered state of being departed from the fixed allotment, for the allotted days of her life were more, and through the occurrence mentioned her years came to the sum stated in the passage. And his saying <b>חַיֵּי שָׂרָה</b> — because the righteous give life to their days, whereas the wicked — their days give life to them." "<b>And further,</b> his saying <b>שְׁנֵי חַיֵּי שָׂרָה</b>: according to what I explained — that he said ״וַיִּהְיוּ״ to indicate that she died on account of distress — the verse informs by saying ״שְׁנֵי חַיֵּי״ etc., meaning that she completed the years of her life that were decreed for her, except that this was the cause through which she died. And according to what I explained — that through the cause of the tidings her years were diminished from what they would have been — the intention is to make known the judgment of kindness that the Holy One, blessed be He, executes for His beloved: for every righteous person who did not complete his days for a known reason, HaShem does not deduct from the righteous what he would have gained had he lived his full years, and He completes his reward for the years in which he did not perform anything (Tanhuma, Ki Tisa 3), for He says to him: had I lived, my desire would have been to acquire merit through good deeds. And this is His saying <b>שְׁנֵי חַיֵּי שָׂרָה</b> — He alludes to the remainder, that all her years were life from her and shone with her glory." "<b>Further,</b> the verse may be explained along this path: <b>And the life of Sarah was</b> — meaning, in distress were the life of Sarah one hundred years, for in all of them she was in distress. Until ninety years she was in the birth pangs of not bearing a child for Abraham — go out and learn from what Rachel said (Genesis 30:1): ״הָבָה לִּי בָנִים וְאִם אַיִן מֵתָה״ etc.; and from ninety to one hundred, approximately, Ishmael was pursuing Isaac to kill him, as they of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 53), and she was distressed over the matter — behold, one hundred years. And twenty years and seven years — these shall be reckoned as שְׁנֵי חַיֵּי שָׂרָה, the years of the life of Sarah, for then she was at peace regarding her son, since Abraham had already driven out Ishmael."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:55.836344586Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}